The Rust crates it links are MIT or Apache-2.0 licensed; Cargo.lock lists them.

At run time the binary loads numpy's bundled OpenBLAS (libscipy_openblas64_*, BSD-3-Clause)
from the Python environment it is installed into; that library is not distributed here.

fibrs predict runs Qual's AFV fibre model V2
(https://huggingface.co/Qualzz20/afv_fiber_9um_v2). The model and its weights are Qual's:
fibrs ships neither and reads the snapshot you point it at. At run time it loads NVIDIA's
cuDNN and cuBLAS from the system or from NVIDIA's pip packages; they are not distributed here.

GPU trace finishing can load NVIDIA's nvCOMP 6 (libnvcomp.so.6) at run time, from NVIDIA's
own PyPI package nvidia-libnvcomp-cu12 (installed by the gpu-zstd extra) or a path given in
FIBRS_NVCOMP, to decode prediction slabs on the GPU. nvCOMP is proprietary, licensed by NVIDIA
under its own SDK licence agreement, and is not distributed here; without it fibrs decodes
on the CPU.

Its tiling, padding, blend, quantisation, tracing downsample and, in --mode villa, its choice
of tiles and output chunks follow villa's lasagna.qualzz_inference and
lasagna.tiled_predict3d (https://github.com/ScrollPrize/villa, commit 97fb9336c);
src/predict/villa.rs reimplements villa's tile-selection rule. villa is MIT licensed,
Copyright (c) 2024 Vesuvius Challenge; the full text is in licenses/villa-MIT.txt.

On Linux x86-64 the binary includes fibrs predict's engine, volkit, whose embedded GPU kernels
contain compiled code from NVIDIA libdevice (CUDA Toolkit 13.2), distributed under the NVIDIA
CUDA Toolkit End User License Agreement and not under this crate's licence, and code compiled
from NVIDIA's cuda-oxide device crate (Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2024-2026 NVIDIA CORPORATION
& AFFILIATES). licenses/nvidia-gpu-kernels.txt gives the details and the terms that apply.
